1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,150
The sentence is said in a planning meeting,
with confidence, and a reorganization of

2
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:13,784
the team's workflow is about to be decided
on it. If you agree, you have endorsed a

3
00:00:13,824 --> 00:00:18,251
claim the research doesn't make. If you
answer "that is only a survey", you have

4
00:00:18,291 --> 00:00:21,042
dismissed evidence that
does say something useful.

5
00:00:21,382 --> 00:00:26,215
The honest answer has three parts: what was
measured, how, and what it doesn't show.

6
00:00:26,255 --> 00:00:30,623
And it ends with a variable the team
can act on. Keep that variable in mind.

7
00:00:30,663 --> 00:00:33,175
You'll have it word for
word before the end.

8
00:00:35,015 --> 00:00:39,199
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

9
00:00:39,239 --> 00:00:44,807
One reminder first: a branch is a named
line of work, and a merge brings one line

10
00:00:44,847 --> 00:00:50,001
into another. Video 171 described the
models and video 172 ran two of them.

11
00:00:50,041 --> 00:00:55,817
Two questions are left. First: what do teams
use so that one long-lived branch stays

12
00:00:55,857 --> 00:01:00,456
workable, when features take weeks
and many people merge every day?

13
00:01:00,796 --> 00:01:04,390
That's feature flags, merge
queues and stacked changes.

14
00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:09,609
You met the queue in video 129 and the
stack in video 126 as GitHub mechanisms.

15
00:01:09,649 --> 00:01:15,093
Here they're placed in the strategy discussion.
Second: how do you choose, and what

16
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:17,273
may you claim when you recommend?

17
00:01:17,613 --> 00:01:22,523
This video has no new commands. Its terminal
segment is short and has one purpose:

18
00:01:22,563 --> 00:01:26,085
to show the kind of evidence a
recommendation can point at.

19
00:01:26,125 --> 00:01:29,827
The rest is a decision table,
used live on one described team.

20
00:01:31,667 --> 00:01:33,246
Here's what you'll walk away with.

21
00:01:33,586 --> 00:01:34,998
After this video you can:

22
00:01:35,338 --> 00:01:38,511
Explain what a feature flag
separates and what it costs.

23
00:01:38,851 --> 00:01:43,370
Say what a merge queue guarantees that
"require branches to be up to date" does not.

24
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:47,888
State what the DORA research shows about
trunk-based development, how it was

25
00:01:47,928 --> 00:01:49,886
measured, and what it does not show.

26
00:01:50,226 --> 00:01:54,291
Recommend a workflow for a described team
and state the evidence for and against.

27
00:01:54,631 --> 00:01:57,107
Say it to a CTO in four sentences.

28
00:01:58,947 --> 00:02:03,701
Feature flags. A feature flag is a condition
in the code that keeps unfinished or

29
00:02:03,741 --> 00:02:08,436
unreleased behavior switched off, so that
the code can be merged long before the

30
00:02:08,476 --> 00:02:09,619
feature is released.

31
00:02:09,959 --> 00:02:13,897
A flag replaces a long-lived branch
with a short-lived condition.

32
00:02:13,937 --> 00:02:19,038
Instead of keeping feature/batch-api unmerged
for three weeks, you merge it in small

33
00:02:19,078 --> 00:02:23,934
pieces behind if flags.enabled("batch_api"),
and main contains the code from the

34
00:02:23,974 --> 00:02:28,096
first day. In other words, a flag
separates deployment from release.

35
00:02:28,136 --> 00:02:33,115
Deployment is the code reaching production.
Release is the behavior being switched

36
00:02:33,155 --> 00:02:33,910
on for users.

37
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:39,241
Pete Hodgson's taxonomy distinguishes
release toggles, which ship incomplete code

38
00:02:39,281 --> 00:02:43,029
dark, experiment toggles, ops
toggles and permission toggles.

39
00:02:43,069 --> 00:02:47,128
They differ in how long they live
and how dynamically they change.

40
00:02:47,168 --> 00:02:52,345
And the taxonomy names the cost: added
complexity, combinations to test, and "toggle

41
00:02:52,385 --> 00:02:57,003
debt" when flags aren't removed. Microsoft
describes flags as what lets its

42
00:02:57,043 --> 00:03:01,661
developers avoid long-running feature
branches and separate deployment from

43
00:03:01,701 --> 00:03:02,220
exposure.

44
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:07,143
Remember the "extra failure mode" of
GitHub Flow in video 172's table: a

45
00:03:07,183 --> 00:03:10,674
half-finished feature on main
blocks an urgent release.

46
00:03:10,714 --> 00:03:15,939
A flag is what removes it. A half-finished
feature on main doesn't block a release

47
00:03:15,979 --> 00:03:16,774
if it's dark.

48
00:03:17,114 --> 00:03:22,113
When not to use one. Fowler's caveat is
that with flags the unfinished code ships

49
00:03:22,153 --> 00:03:27,277
inside the product, so the approach demands
strong automated tests to keep the main

50
00:03:27,317 --> 00:03:32,441
line healthy. A flag is the wrong tool for
a change that can't be made conditional,

51
00:03:32,481 --> 00:03:35,614
such as a schema migration
or a dependency upgrade.

52
00:03:35,654 --> 00:03:39,098
A flag that guards a security
boundary must fail closed.

53
00:03:39,138 --> 00:03:44,262
And a flag is configuration: configuration
that changes behavior belongs in version

54
00:03:44,302 --> 00:03:47,248
control or in a system
with its own audit trail.

55
00:03:47,588 --> 00:03:52,429
Merge queues. A pull request is GitHub's
object for proposing a merge, and its

56
00:03:52,469 --> 00:03:57,684
checks are the automated tests that run on
it. Two pull requests can each pass their

57
00:03:57,724 --> 00:04:01,000
checks against an older main
and break main together.

58
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:06,005
Requiring every branch to be up to date
before merging fixes that, and creates a

59
00:04:06,045 --> 00:04:09,947
race, because every merge makes
every other pull request stale.

60
00:04:10,287 --> 00:04:14,782
A merge queue runs the required checks on
temporary branches that contain the base

61
00:04:14,822 --> 00:04:17,990
plus the queued changes, in
order, and merges what passes.

62
00:04:18,030 --> 00:04:22,359
That's the guarantee the up-to-date rule
doesn't give without the race: what is

63
00:04:22,399 --> 00:04:25,401
merged was tested in the
combination in which it lands.

64
00:04:25,441 --> 00:04:29,936
On GitHub those checks run on the merge_group
event, and the merge method is fixed

65
00:04:29,976 --> 00:04:32,922
by the queue instead of being
chosen per pull request.

66
00:04:33,262 --> 00:04:36,741
The published experience comes
from large repositories.

67
00:04:36,781 --> 00:04:41,668
GitHub reports about 2,500 pull requests
a month from more than 500 engineers

68
00:04:41,708 --> 00:04:46,594
landing through its own queue, with the
average wait to ship down 33 percent.

69
00:04:46,634 --> 00:04:51,904
Shopify described running CI on a predicted
post-merge branch and ejecting failures

70
00:04:51,944 --> 00:04:56,383
to keep its main branch green. Those
numbers describe those companies.

71
00:04:56,423 --> 00:05:01,374
A team that merges five pull requests a
day gets the correctness guarantee and

72
00:05:01,414 --> 00:05:02,141
little else.

73
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:07,598
Stacked changes. A stack is a chain of
small changes, each reviewed separately and

74
00:05:07,638 --> 00:05:12,755
each based on the one below. The practice
comes from tools with per-commit review,

75
00:05:12,795 --> 00:05:16,906
such as Phabricator at Facebook
and chained changelists at Google.

76
00:05:16,946 --> 00:05:20,805
The stated benefits are an
unblocked author and small reviews.

77
00:05:20,845 --> 00:05:26,025
The stated costs are the rebasing skill and
tooling it needs, and little gain for a

78
00:05:26,065 --> 00:05:31,245
small team. GitHub's native stacked pull
requests have been in public preview since

79
00:05:31,285 --> 00:05:32,943
the thirtieth of July 2026.

80
00:05:33,283 --> 00:05:36,403
Neither a queue nor a stack
is a branching strategy.

81
00:05:36,443 --> 00:05:41,507
A merge queue makes any model with pre-merge
checks safer at volume, and stacks make

82
00:05:41,547 --> 00:05:45,700
short-lived branches practical for
a change too large for one review.

83
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:50,588
What the evidence shows. The research
associates short-lived branches and small

84
00:05:50,628 --> 00:05:55,293
batches with better delivery performance.
It doesn't rank named workflows, and it

85
00:05:55,333 --> 00:05:56,687
doesn't establish cause.

86
00:05:57,027 --> 00:06:01,997
DORA is a research programme on software
delivery, and its findings come from

87
00:06:02,037 --> 00:06:07,332
surveys. DORA defines trunk-based development
as each developer dividing work into

88
00:06:07,372 --> 00:06:11,301
small batches and merging into
the trunk at least once a day.

89
00:06:11,341 --> 00:06:16,637
Its capability page reports, from research
conducted in 2016 and 2017, that higher

90
00:06:16,677 --> 00:06:21,972
delivery performance is associated with
three or fewer active branches, merging to

91
00:06:22,012 --> 00:06:26,136
trunk at least daily, and no code
freezes or integration phases.

92
00:06:26,476 --> 00:06:31,331
It lists heavyweight, slow code review and
skipping automated tests before commit as

93
00:06:31,371 --> 00:06:35,759
pitfalls. A companion capability says
that working in small batches predicts

94
00:06:35,799 --> 00:06:38,090
delivery and organizational performance.

95
00:06:38,430 --> 00:06:44,173
Read the left column: survey-based,
correlational, expressed in branch lifetime and

96
00:06:44,213 --> 00:06:47,099
batch size, and dating from 2016 and 2017.

97
00:06:47,439 --> 00:06:51,545
Any specific number for how much
trunk-based development improves delivery

98
00:06:51,585 --> 00:06:56,196
performance. The notes behind this course
contain none, and you shouldn't quote one

99
00:06:56,236 --> 00:06:56,869
from memory.

100
00:06:57,209 --> 00:07:00,524
Quick quiz. The research
says "is associated with".

101
00:07:00,564 --> 00:07:05,986
Which sentence may you say in the meeting?
A, trunk-based development causes better

102
00:07:06,026 --> 00:07:11,315
delivery. B, teams that report short-lived
branches and small batches also report

103
00:07:11,355 --> 00:07:15,000
better delivery performance.
C, Git Flow performs worst.

104
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:15,790
Your answer?

105
00:07:20,630 --> 00:07:24,522
B. Sentence A claims a cause, and
the research is correlational.

106
00:07:24,562 --> 00:07:28,024
Sentence C names a workflow,
and the research ranks none.

107
00:07:28,364 --> 00:07:32,752
What other sources add. Fowler, who
accepts this research, still lists real

108
00:07:32,792 --> 00:07:37,535
advantages of feature branching: a feature
can be assessed as a unit, code enters

109
00:07:37,575 --> 00:07:42,376
the product only when complete, and it
suits teams that can't yet keep a main line

110
00:07:42,416 --> 00:07:46,863
healthy and open-source projects with
occasional contributors who aren't yet

111
00:07:46,903 --> 00:07:47,335
trusted.

112
00:07:47,675 --> 00:07:52,666
An interview-and-survey study of Brazilian
developers published in 2025 concludes

113
00:07:52,706 --> 00:07:57,884
that trunk-based workflows suit fast-paced
projects with experienced, smaller teams,

114
00:07:57,924 --> 00:08:02,791
and branch-based workflows suit less
experienced and larger teams despite their

115
00:08:02,831 --> 00:08:06,145
management overhead. It's
one study of one population.

116
00:08:06,185 --> 00:08:11,300
The company case studies come from organizations
with very large investments in CI,

117
00:08:11,340 --> 00:08:15,834
flags and tooling, and Google and Meta
don't use stock Git for their main

118
00:08:15,874 --> 00:08:20,741
repositories, so they support the principle
more than any Git command sequence.

119
00:08:21,081 --> 00:08:25,628
DORA's recent reports describe AI as an
amplifier of an organization's existing

120
00:08:25,668 --> 00:08:30,157
strengths and weaknesses, and keep small
batches and version control among the

121
00:08:30,197 --> 00:08:31,609
capabilities that matter.

122
00:08:31,949 --> 00:08:36,812
How to say it to a CTO. The textbook's
wording: "The research does not prove that

123
00:08:36,852 --> 00:08:41,836
trunk-based development is best. It shows
that teams reporting short-lived branches

124
00:08:41,876 --> 00:08:45,347
and small batches also report
better delivery performance.

125
00:08:45,387 --> 00:08:50,310
The variable we can act on is how long our
branches live and how large our changes

126
00:08:50,350 --> 00:08:55,334
are, under whatever name we give the model."
There's the variable from the opening:

127
00:08:55,374 --> 00:08:57,271
branch lifetime and change size.

128
00:08:57,611 --> 00:09:02,889
Choosing by context. No strategy is best.
The reputable sources tie the choice to

129
00:09:02,929 --> 00:09:08,141
context, and two questions come before
all others: how many versions are live at

130
00:09:08,181 --> 00:09:10,439
once, and how often do you release.

131
00:09:10,779 --> 00:09:16,247
Try it now, on paper, for thirty seconds.
Think of a project you know, and write two

132
00:09:16,287 --> 00:09:20,837
answers. How many versions are live
at once? How often do you release?

133
00:09:20,877 --> 00:09:21,493
I'll wait.

134
00:09:26,333 --> 00:09:31,193
Keep that paper. Those two answers choose
your first rows in the decision table that

135
00:09:31,233 --> 00:09:33,876
is coming up, and more
than one row is normal.

136
00:09:35,716 --> 00:09:40,495
Three pictures, one for each topic. For
flags, the textbook's analogy is a new wing

137
00:09:40,535 --> 00:09:45,256
of a building, built and inspected behind
a locked door: part of the building from

138
00:09:45,296 --> 00:09:50,133
day one, open to the public later. The
analogy breaks because both sides of the door

139
00:09:50,173 --> 00:09:55,009
run in the same production process, so a
mistake behind the door can still bring the

140
00:09:55,049 --> 00:09:55,822
building down.

141
00:09:56,162 --> 00:10:01,080
For evidence, hold a ladder with three
rungs. On the lowest rung: "these companies

142
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:05,977
describe doing this." Above it: "teams
that report this practice also report that

143
00:10:06,017 --> 00:10:10,995
outcome." On the top rung, which this
research doesn't reach: "this practice causes

144
00:10:11,035 --> 00:10:15,711
that outcome, by this much." When you
cite, say which rung you're standing on.

145
00:10:15,751 --> 00:10:17,888
Stop at the rung the source reached.

146
00:10:18,228 --> 00:10:22,820
For the choice, think of the decision
table as a set of pressures, not a lookup.

147
00:10:22,860 --> 00:10:27,047
A team is usually in several rows at
once, and the rows push in different

148
00:10:27,087 --> 00:10:31,796
directions. The design is what you get when
you name each pressure and the control

149
00:10:31,836 --> 00:10:32,722
that answers it.

150
00:10:34,562 --> 00:10:39,603
With the flag, the feature never exists
as a diverged line, so it never needs a

151
00:10:39,643 --> 00:10:42,111
large merge and never blocks a release.

152
00:10:42,451 --> 00:10:47,223
And the stack of three pull requests:
each one names the one below as its base.

153
00:10:47,563 --> 00:10:50,992
And the decision table of section 27.14.

154
00:10:51,332 --> 00:10:55,706
Each row is a context, what it pushes
you toward, why, and what to watch.

155
00:10:57,546 --> 00:11:03,240
Into the lab, briefly. It's the work of
video 172 again, done under two strategies

156
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:07,295
in two repositories, and every
command only reads history.

157
00:11:07,635 --> 00:11:10,162
Step 1: the result under GitHub Flow.

158
00:11:10,502 --> 00:11:14,052
Two commits between the tags:
the fix and the batch endpoint.

159
00:11:14,392 --> 00:11:19,797
Step 2: the result with a release branch.
Predict the last line before it appears.

160
00:11:19,837 --> 00:11:20,860
Say it out loud.

161
00:11:25,700 --> 00:11:27,195
One commit between the tags.

162
00:11:27,535 --> 00:11:30,844
Step 3: the same question
asked of both repositories.

163
00:11:31,184 --> 00:11:34,848
This is the form of evidence to
bring to a strategy discussion.

164
00:11:35,188 --> 00:11:40,184
Not "release branches are cleaner", but:
under this model the patch release changes

165
00:11:40,224 --> 00:11:45,219
two files, under that one a single file.
This model leaves one long-lived ref, that

166
00:11:45,259 --> 00:11:49,222
one leaves two. Each line is a
command anyone in the room can run.

167
00:11:49,562 --> 00:11:54,665
Take this description: a company of forty
engineers that runs a hosted LLM gateway,

168
00:11:54,705 --> 00:11:59,684
deployed continuously, and from next quarter
also ships an on-premises edition to

169
00:11:59,724 --> 00:12:04,702
two customers who stay one version behind.
It's subject to an audit that asks who

170
00:12:04,742 --> 00:12:09,721
approved each production change. Before
we go down the table, predict: which rows

171
00:12:09,761 --> 00:12:12,075
apply to this team? Say them out loud.

172
00:12:16,915 --> 00:12:22,299
Now down the rows. Row one applies: one
live hosted version, deployed continuously,

173
00:12:22,339 --> 00:12:25,893
so one long-lived branch and
flags for unfinished work.

174
00:12:25,933 --> 00:12:31,056
Row three applies from next quarter:
several supported versions, so release/x.y

175
00:12:31,096 --> 00:12:36,219
branches, a fix direction that is written
down, and the port check as a release

176
00:12:36,259 --> 00:12:41,381
gate. Row seven applies: a regulated
environment, so required review by someone

177
00:12:41,421 --> 00:12:45,433
other than the author, signed
tags, and bypasses made visible.

178
00:12:45,773 --> 00:12:50,006
Three rows at once, and the
textbook says so: the rows combine.

179
00:12:50,046 --> 00:12:55,704
We won't resolve it into a named model here.
The design itself is the subject of Lab

180
00:12:55,744 --> 00:13:01,130
34.1 and of video 179, and you write it
yourself. What you take from here is the

181
00:13:01,170 --> 00:13:06,760
method: name the rows, and for each row say
what it pushes toward, why, and what to

182
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:07,167
watch.

183
00:13:07,507 --> 00:13:12,476
One more sentence from the section, for the
day somebody asks whether the choice is

184
00:13:12,516 --> 00:13:17,244
final. Changing the model later is cheap
in Git, because branches are refs, and

185
00:13:17,284 --> 00:13:22,192
expensive in habits, pipelines and rulesets.
So start with the simplest model that

186
00:13:22,232 --> 00:13:27,080
answers the two questions, and add a branch
only when you can name the version or

187
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:29,253
the audit requirement that needs it.

188
00:13:31,093 --> 00:13:32,741
Five mistakes to watch for.

189
00:13:33,081 --> 00:13:36,102
Citing DORA as proof that
a named workflow is best.

190
00:13:36,142 --> 00:13:40,785
Root cause: the research is survey-based
and correlational and is expressed in

191
00:13:40,825 --> 00:13:44,026
branch lifetime and batch
size, not in workflow names.

192
00:13:44,366 --> 00:13:49,277
Quoting an effect size from memory. Root
cause: the source material behind this

193
00:13:49,317 --> 00:13:53,100
course contains no such number,
so any figure is unsupported.

194
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:58,517
Never removing flags. Root cause: each flag
adds states to test, and a flag without

195
00:13:58,557 --> 00:14:03,572
an owner and an end date becomes permanent
configuration, which the taxonomy calls

196
00:14:03,612 --> 00:14:04,312
toggle debt.

197
00:14:04,652 --> 00:14:09,444
Adopting a merge queue for its throughput
numbers. Root cause: the published numbers

198
00:14:09,484 --> 00:14:14,104
describe very large repositories; a small
team gets the correctness guarantee and

199
00:14:14,144 --> 00:14:14,794
little else.

200
00:14:15,134 --> 00:14:19,938
Calling a queue or a stack a branching
strategy. Root cause: both are techniques for

201
00:14:19,978 --> 00:14:24,724
integrating and reviewing under any model
with pre-merge checks; they do not decide

202
00:14:24,764 --> 00:14:26,570
which long-lived branches exist.

203
00:14:28,410 --> 00:14:33,094
Now, out of the lab. An LLM application
team wants to change the default prompt of

204
00:14:33,134 --> 00:14:37,472
its support assistant. The change touches
the prompt template, the retrieval

205
00:14:37,512 --> 00:14:41,793
settings and the evaluation thresholds,
and the work will take three weeks.

206
00:14:41,833 --> 00:14:46,229
The old way was a long-lived branch and
a merge that nobody wanted to review.

207
00:14:46,569 --> 00:14:49,804
The team merges the work in
small pieces behind a flag.

208
00:14:49,844 --> 00:14:54,745
The natural flags for an LLM application
are the model name, the prompt version and

209
00:14:54,785 --> 00:14:59,568
the retrieval settings. The new prompt is
merged, then evaluated on a fraction of

210
00:14:59,608 --> 00:15:04,509
traffic before it becomes the default. Two
consequences follow, and the lead states

211
00:15:04,549 --> 00:15:05,938
both in the design note.

212
00:15:06,278 --> 00:15:11,123
First, "which prompt answered this ticket"
is now a question about a flag value at a

213
00:15:11,163 --> 00:15:15,658
time, not only about a commit, so the
flag value is recorded with each answer.

214
00:15:15,698 --> 00:15:20,543
Second, the flag has an owner and a removal
date, and the evaluation suite runs with

215
00:15:20,583 --> 00:15:22,810
the flag on and with it off until then.

216
00:15:24,650 --> 00:15:31,449
Your turn. Do Exercise 32.2, Level
2, "Six teams, which model?", in

217
00:15:31,489 --> 00:15:34,511
exercises/m32-m34-practice.md.

218
00:15:34,851 --> 00:15:39,350
For each team, before you name anything,
write down your answers to the two

219
00:15:39,390 --> 00:15:44,009
questions: how many versions are live
at once, and how often do they release.

220
00:15:44,049 --> 00:15:48,849
Then predict which rows of the table apply,
and only then make a recommendation,

221
00:15:48,889 --> 00:15:52,783
with one sentence of evidence for
it and one sentence against it.

222
00:15:52,823 --> 00:15:57,563
The solutions are in a separate file.
Open them after you have written all six.

223
00:15:57,903 --> 00:16:04,512
The challenge is Exercise 32.7, Level 5,
"The regression in 3.0", in the same file.

224
00:16:08,352 --> 00:16:12,712
Answer out loud. A strong answer keeps
the three parts of the question apart.

225
00:16:12,752 --> 00:16:17,397
For "what does it show", it states the
association in the terms the research uses:

226
00:16:17,437 --> 00:16:22,083
which practices, which outcome. For "how
was it measured", it names the method and

227
00:16:22,123 --> 00:16:26,197
the years, and draws the consequence
for the verb you're allowed to use.

228
00:16:26,537 --> 00:16:31,147
For "what would you say instead", it
gives a sentence a CTO can act on, with a

229
00:16:31,187 --> 00:16:36,154
variable the team controls. It neither
overstates the evidence nor dismisses it, and

230
00:16:36,194 --> 00:16:37,764
it doesn't invent a number.

231
00:16:39,604 --> 00:16:41,653
Let's land this, in your own words.

232
00:16:41,993 --> 00:16:47,060
A feature flag separates deployment from
release; it costs added states to test and

233
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:48,721
flags that must be removed.

234
00:16:49,061 --> 00:16:53,627
A merge queue tests each change in the
combination in which it will land, which the

235
00:16:53,667 --> 00:16:56,346
up-to-date requirement
achieves only with a race.

236
00:16:56,686 --> 00:17:01,440
The DORA findings are survey-based and
correlational and are about branch lifetime

237
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:05,357
and batch size; they rank no named
workflow and establish no cause.

238
00:17:05,697 --> 00:17:10,593
The two first questions for any strategy
are how many versions are live at once and

239
00:17:10,633 --> 00:17:14,280
how often you release; the rows
of the decision table combine.

240
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:19,280
Start with the simplest model that answers
the two questions, and add a branch only

241
00:17:19,320 --> 00:17:23,187
when you can name the version or the
audit requirement that needs it.

242
00:17:25,027 --> 00:17:32,898
Read sections 27.11 to 27.14. Then do
Exercise 32.6, Level 3, "Say it to a CTO", in

243
00:17:32,938 --> 00:17:40,714
exercises/m32-m34-practice.md: four sentences,
spoken, recorded, and compared with

244
00:17:40,754 --> 00:17:43,097
the section the next day.

245
00:17:43,437 --> 00:17:48,028
Today you learned to say exactly what the
evidence supports, and to stop there.

246
00:17:48,068 --> 00:17:51,662
That's a rare skill in a planning
meeting, and it's yours now.

247
00:17:51,702 --> 00:17:56,469
Say your four sentences out loud once. The
next video gives the reason behind each

248
00:17:56,509 --> 00:18:01,100
team practice and the root cause behind
each anti-pattern, with code review and

249
00:18:01,140 --> 00:18:05,790
commit message conventions. Until then,
look at the state first and type second.

250
00:18:05,830 --> 00:18:07,197
See you in the next one.
